drumee-bootstrap (2.0.5) stable; urgency=medium

  * entrypoint/web: do not override the pid path on the command line. 2.0.4 passed
    `-g "pid /tmp/nginx.pid;"` so nginx could run as uid 8000, and nginx refused the
    configuration outright: `"pid" directive is duplicate in /etc/nginx/nginx.conf:4`.
    A -g directive is additive, not an override. Ownership is the fix — the image now
    pre-creates /run/nginx.pid owned by that uid, along with /var/lib/nginx and
    /var/log/nginx — and `nginx -t` runs with exactly the arguments the exec uses, so
    the check and the run cannot disagree.


  * lib.sh: source /etc/drumee/drumee.sh with `set -u` OFF. That file is generated per
    deployment and legitimately references variables it does not define —
    OWN_CERTS_DIR among them — so under `set -u` every entrypoint that calls
    drumee_env() aborted with "OWN_CERTS_DIR: parameter not set" before starting
    anything. Measured on the web role against a real rendered tree; the same trap has
    bitten the native install harness for the same reason, which is why it is fixed in
    the one shared place rather than in each role.


  * entrypoint/web: start nginx as the uid the role actually runs as. Debian's nginx
    expects to start as root and drop to www-data; this role runs as uid 8000, so
    nginx is master AND worker and every path it writes must belong to that uid. It
    crash-looped on `mkdir() "/var/lib/nginx/body" failed (13: Permission denied)`
    and, once ownership was fixed in the image, on the pid file in root-owned /run.
    The pid is the one of the three that ownership cannot fix, so it is redirected
    with -g, which wins over nginx.conf without editing a distro conffile.
    `nginx -t` now runs with the same -g as the exec, so the check and the run cannot
    disagree.

 -- packaging@drumee.com  Sat, 08 Aug 2026 08:33:03 +0200

drumee-bootstrap (2.0.2) stable; urgency=medium

  * entrypoint/infra: clear the 'auto' address sentinel from the ENVIRONMENT, not
    just from the flags. 2.0.1 stopped passing --public-ip4=auto and a zone file
    named `auto` was still produced, because infra.js reads PUBLIC_IP4 from the
    environment as well (templates/utils.js) and the env value wins over detection.
    Omitting the flag was not enough. render.mjs no longer emits the sentinel either,
    but this entrypoint now stays correct whatever set it.

 -- packaging@drumee.com  Sat, 08 Aug 2026 08:26:31 +0200

drumee-bootstrap (2.0.1) stable; urgency=medium

  * entrypoint/infra: the real implementation. It was a 16-line skeleton that ran
    `infra.js --chroot="$OUT" --only-infra=1` with OUT defaulting to /etc/drumee —
    which would have written the tree to /etc/drumee/etc/drumee — and passed neither
    the domain nor any address, so it could not have rendered a usable
    configuration for any deployment.
    .
    It now carries what building the role image actually taught: the domain and
    address flags infra.js declares (and only those — --admin-email is not one, and
    an undeclared flag makes argparse exit 2 with a usage dump that reads like a
    crash); DKIM generation before the render, because the zone embeds the public
    half; self-signed certificate creation via setup-infra's own
    bin/create-local-certs, without which nginx in the web role has nothing to load
    and refuses to start; ownership fixed to 8000:8000 with credentials 0640 inside
    a 0750 directory; and idempotence delegated to infra.js's own
    hasExistingSettings(), so a restarted job is a no-op and FORCE_RENDER=1 is the
    one flag that re-renders — deliberately the same contract as dpkg-reconfigure on
    the native channel.
    .
    PUBLIC_IP4/6 of 'auto' are IGNORED rather than forwarded. 'auto' is drumee.yaml's
    sentinel for "detect at install time" and render.mjs emits it verbatim into .env;
    forwarding it produced a BIND zone file literally named `auto` and a bogus
    01-public.conf. Both observed in a running stack. A container cannot resolve
    'auto' anyway — it sees a bridge address, so detection would publish an address
    nothing can reach.
    .
    It deliberately does NOT call drumee_env(): that sources
    /etc/drumee/drumee.sh, which is a file this job writes, and reading a previous
    render's output before producing the next one lets a stale value survive a
    deliberate reconfiguration.

 -- packaging@drumee.com  Sat, 08 Aug 2026 08:21:36 +0200

drumee-bootstrap (2.0.0) trixie; urgency=medium

  * Initial package under this name for the container distribution: per-role
    entrypoints (app, web, media, dns, mail, schemas, infra), per-role
    healthchecks, and a shared library for credential provisioning.
  * Starts at 2.0.0 rather than 0.1.0: the name was used until 1.2.6 by the
    interactive installer, since renamed to drumee-installer. Reusing a package
    name is only safe while the version line keeps rising.

